{"id":101,"date":"2026-07-09T14:25:53","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:25:53","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/smarter-thermostats-lower-bills-a-norristown-field-guide\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:55","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:55","slug":"smarter-thermostats-lower-bills-a-norristown-field-guide","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/smarter-thermostats-lower-bills-a-norristown-field-guide\/","title":{"rendered":"Norristown Carved Houses Into Apartments \u2014 Nobody Fixed the Ducts"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream s-hero-text s-band--spacious has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center s-lead has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Norristown&#039;s multi-family conversions have carved up single-family homes into rental units without rethinking the airflow.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">How do you heat three units?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Book the Assessment<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"#content\">Our Services<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Quick Answers for Multi-Unit Buildings<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--numbered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I split a single-family home into rental units without upgrading the HVAC?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can, but you&#039;ll likely have comfort and cost-allocation problems. A single HVAC system with one thermostat can&#039;t serve multiple independent households fairly. Tenants will fight over the temperature, utility costs will be hard to divide, and someone will always be uncomfortable. The right approach is to install separate HVAC systems for each unit\u2014typically ductless mini-splits\u2014so every tenant has their own thermostat and their own utility bill.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It&#039;s a larger upfront investment, but it makes the units more marketable and eliminates ongoing disputes.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why is my second-floor unit always hotter or colder than the first floor?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Because hot air rises and the HVAC system isn&#039;t zoned for multi-unit occupancy. In a conversion with a shared heating system, the heat will naturally migrate to the upper floors, leaving the first floor cold in winter. In summer, cool air sinks, so the first floor stays comfortable while the second floor overheats.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The solution is to give each floor its own HVAC system or to install zoning dampers that can direct airflow to the unit that&#039;s calling for conditioning. Without that, you&#039;re fighting the laws of physics, and the physics will win.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What&#039;s the best way to allocate heating and cooling costs in a multi-unit building?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Meter each unit separately so tenants pay for the energy they actually use. This requires unit-level HVAC systems\u2014typically mini-splits with dedicated electrical circuits for each unit. If that&#039;s not feasible, submetering is the next best option, followed by a ratio-based allocation system that accounts for square footage and unit characteristics. Avoid flat splits or including costs in the rent unless you&#039;re willing to absorb the financial risk and the complaints from tenants who feel they&#039;re subsidizing their neighbors.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why does a single system fail in multi-unit homes?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A single-family home with one thermostat works because everyone in the house theoretically cooperates\u2014they agree on a temperature, they open or close doors to balance comfort, and they share heating costs. In a multi-unit building, none of that is true. The first-floor tenant wants it warm and turns up the thermostat, which causes the second-floor unit to overheat because hot air rises and the system can&#039;t distinguish between zones.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The second-floor tenant opens windows to cool down, which increases the heating load and makes the first-floor tenant cold. The third-floor unit swelters in summer because the AC can&#039;t push cool air up two flights of stairs, or freezes in winter because the heat all stays downstairs.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No one is comfortable, everyone is frustrated, and utility bills are enormous because the system runs constantly trying to satisfy a thermostat that only represents one unit&#039;s needs. Multi-family conversions also create spaces without access to original ductwork or radiators. A second-floor apartment carved from two bedrooms and a hallway might have a kitchenette in a closet and a bathroom in a small den.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Those new spaces need conditioning, but the original HVAC system has no registers there. The landlord either leaves them unconditioned or installs window units as a band-aid. Neither solves the underlying problem that the home&#039;s infrastructure was never designed to support multiple separate living units.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b.jpg\" alt=\"attic insulation\" class=\"wp-image-190\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/46991127814_83c79f9a9e_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Mice holes in attic insulation&quot; by jaredhouliston, CC0, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What HVAC solutions work for multi-unit conversions?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-feature-row is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg\" alt=\"attic insulation\" class=\"wp-image-189\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/3145968830_73d42a7d2e_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;The R11 attic insulation&quot; by zieak, BY, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ductless Mini-Splits<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Each unit gets independent temperature control and separate metering. Fast installation, no ductwork needed, efficient cooling and heating. Standard solution for Norristown conversions.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1478\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-ductwork-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zoned Ducted Systems<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Central equipment with motorized dampers directs airflow to active zones only. Works if ductwork is in good condition and building is two-unit conversion. Requires careful balancing.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png\" alt=\"ductwork - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1759\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-ductwork-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Airflow Modifications<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Transfer grilles, jump ducts, balancing dampers, and return-air paths improve circulation through subdivided spaces. Often combined with zoning or mini-split systems for better performance.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1476\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-2.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Utility Metering<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Separate electrical or gas meters for each unit ensure fair cost allocation and tenant control. Essential for tenant satisfaction and landlord risk management.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-minisplit-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"minisplit - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1481\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-minisplit-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-minisplit-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-minisplit-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-minisplit-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How do ductless mini-splits solve the conversion problem?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ductless mini-split systems have become the standard for multi-family conversions in Norristown because they solve most problems that shared HVAC systems create. Each unit gets its own outdoor condenser and its own indoor heads, so every tenant has independent temperature control and pays their own utility bill for heating and cooling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The systems don&#039;t require ductwork, so installation is fast and minimally invasive\u2014you can add HVAC to a newly created unit without tearing out walls or running new ducts through other tenants&#039; spaces. The equipment is efficient, quiet, and reliable. Because each unit is metered separately, there&#039;s no dispute about who used how much energy.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For a landlord trying to make a conversion pencil out, mini-splits are often the only practical option. However, the design matters enormously. Each unit needs enough indoor heads to cover all living spaces\u2014at minimum, one in the main room and one in the bedroom, with additional heads for larger units or homes with poor airflow.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Outdoor condensers need careful siting so they&#039;re not blocking sidewalks, creating noise complaints, or cluttering the building&#039;s exterior. In a building with three or four units, that means three or four outdoor condensers, which can turn the yard into a mechanical space if not planned thoughtfully.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some buildings cluster condensers on a common pad and screen them with fencing, while others mount them on brackets at different heights on the building&#039;s exterior. Either way, it takes coordination and a willingness to invest in a clean installation rather than just mounting units wherever they fit.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Is your converted building struggling with HVAC?<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Tenant comfort complaints concentrate in specific units or floors\u2014zoning or distribution issue<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Shared system with tenant-controlled thermostats\u2014thermostat wars wasting energy<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Individual unit utility bills vary 40%+ for similar-sized spaces\u2014insulation or equipment disparity<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Vacancy periods causing pipe-freeze risk\u2014smart monitoring needed for unoccupied units<\/li>\n\n\n<li>PECO bills spike when all units are occupied\u2014shared system can&#039;t handle multi-unit load<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What happens when you modify ductwork for zoning?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If a multi-unit building has existing ductwork and the landlord wants to keep a central system rather than switching to mini-splits, the only way to make it work is with aggressive zoning. Each unit becomes a separate zone with its own thermostat and its own set of dampers that control airflow to that unit only.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When the first-floor thermostat calls for heat, the dampers to the first-floor registers open and the dampers to the other floors close, so the heat goes where it&#039;s needed. When the second-floor thermostat calls for cooling, the process reverses. This prevents one tenant from conditioning another tenant&#039;s space and gives everyone independent control.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to solve airflow blockages in subdivided floor plans<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Install Transfer Grilles \u2014 Louvered vents in walls or doors allow air to pass between rooms without compromising privacy. Cheap and easy to install, but transmit sound. Essential in units where bedroom doors are kept closed.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Run Jump Ducts \u2014 Short ductwork sections connect rooms through attic or wall cavities, bypassing doors and walls. More expensive than transfer grilles but eliminates noise transmission. Works best in buildings with accessible attics.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Balance the Ductwork \u2014 Adjust dampers in trunk lines to restrict flow to over-served areas and boost flow to under-served areas. Improves comfort without adding equipment. Prevents distant rooms from running cold or hot.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Add Return-Air Registers \u2014 Each unit needs its own return-air path through dedicated ducts or transfer grilles. Without adequate return air, the system is starved and underperforms. Critical in conversions where new walls block original return paths.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Seal the Building Envelope \u2014 Caulk penetrations, insulate rim joists, seal around windows and doors. Reduces load on HVAC and prevents one tenant&#039;s conditioned air from leaking into a neighbor&#039;s unit or escaping outdoors.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How should utility costs be divided fairly?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One of the most contentious issues in Norristown&#039;s multi-unit conversions is how to allocate heating and cooling costs when the building has a shared HVAC system. Including the cost in the rent removes the tenant&#039;s incentive to conserve energy and shifts the financial risk to the landlord, who has no control over how high tenants set their thermostats.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Splitting the cost evenly among units creates resentment, especially when some units are larger, sunnier, or occupied by people who prefer different temperatures. The only truly fair approach is to meter each unit separately so tenants pay for the energy they actually use. This requires each unit to have its own HVAC equipment, which is why mini-splits are the standard for new conversions.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Submetering is a workaround for existing buildings with central systems\u2014landlords install secondary meters for each unit and bill based on individual consumption. This works for electricity if each unit has its own electrical panel, but it&#039;s harder for gas. Submetering also doesn&#039;t solve the comfort problem, because tenants still don&#039;t have control over their own temperature. It&#039;s better than a flat split, but inferior to unit-level systems.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills\">\n<li>Individual metering with ductless zones eliminates tenant billing disputes<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Converted Victorian mansions need 3-4 separate systems for independent control<\/li>\n\n\n<li>DeKalb Street properties face condenser placement limits due to sidewalk clearance<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Brick rowhomes with original oversized ductwork respond well to right-sized equipment<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Electrical service upgrades often required when adding multiple mini-split systems<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What do successful Norristown conversions have in common?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Norristown&#039;s most successful multi-family conversions are the ones where the owner invests in unit-level systems from the start rather than trying to stretch the existing infrastructure. The homes are typically wood-frame construction from the early 20th century, with plaster walls, minimal insulation, and single-pane windows. Many still have original heating systems\u2014radiators or gravity furnaces\u2014that were adequate for single-family occupancy but can&#039;t handle multiple units with competing temperature preferences.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The brick rowhomes along DeKalb Street and Airy Street near Elmwood Park were converted from coal heat decades ago but most still carry oversized original ductwork and single-zone layout. These properties respond well to right-sized equipment once the duct losses are sealed and airflow paths are optimized.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The key insight is that a conversion isn&#039;t just about adding walls and kitchens\u2014it&#039;s about rethinking the building&#039;s mechanical systems to match the new occupancy pattern. Mini-splits are the most common solution, but ducted systems with proper zoning and adequate return-air paths can work if designed correctly from the beginning.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-cta has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ready to fix your multi-unit comfort problem?<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If your DeKalb Street rowhome has hot and cold spots on every floor, a duct inspection will show us the real problem. Or call and describe what&#039;s happening\u2014we answer comfort questions before scheduling anything.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule the Inspection<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">More HVAC Questions for Property Owners<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--minimal has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>How much does it cost to install mini-split systems in a three-unit conversion?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Costs vary based on unit size, indoor head count, outdoor condenser placement, and electrical upgrades. A typical three-unit building with one head per unit and one outdoor condenser per unit might run $12,000 to $18,000 installed, plus $1,500 to $3,000 for electrical service upgrades if needed. Mini-splits are more expensive upfront than a single central system, but they eliminate future disputes and improve tenant retention.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I use the original furnace if I install mini-splits in some units but not others?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Only if you carve out a separate zone for the remaining units. If you&#039;re installing mini-splits in two units of a three-unit building, the third unit can&#039;t share the original furnace with the other two because you can&#039;t meter or control its airflow independently. That unit needs its own equipment too, or you&#039;re back to a shared-cost problem. The only time a mixed approach works is if the building has truly separate mechanical spaces for each unit.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>What should I look for in an HVAC contractor for a multi-unit project?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Look for someone with experience in multi-family conversions, not just residential HVAC. They need to understand zoning, ductwork design, and electrical integration. Ask to see examples of three or four unit buildings they&#039;ve worked on. A good contractor will evaluate the entire building before designing the system and will coordinate with your electrician to size the service upgrade correctly. Avoid contractors who size equipment on a room-by-room basis without understanding the building&#039;s load patterns.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style